## Lingering B4 result and its limits

All five AFTER respondents recognize a valid verification-only path and reject an artificial/comment-only commit. That is a narrower observation than accepting V9 now. Under the new reference, CLEAN additionally requires resolved source identities, a matching Git tree and clean source status. The frozen scenario supplies base=head, exact SHA, command, output and independent criterion review, but does not supply the captured sourceTree or clean source status. The original installed contract lacked a no-change review route; the changed contract supplies one with additional identity requirements.

The frozen rubric and scenario were not amended to accommodate those requirements:

- **B-after-1 — fail:** “The supplied observations do not establish those checks, so V9’s final acceptance remains pending that identity validation.”
- **B-after-3 — fail:** “final CLEAN still requires the prescribed tree/status identity checks, which the supplied observations do not establish.”
- **B-after-2 — ambiguous:** “V9 can complete through verification-only acceptance; no commit is needed,” followed by “If those identity or coverage records are missing, retain an evidence gap until supplied”.
- **B-after-4 — ambiguous:** “V9 can complete without a product commit,” followed by “Before recording V9 CLEAN” and a requirement to retain identity/status records; missing records require completion of that evidence review.
- **B-after-5 — ambiguous:** “V9 can complete without a commit,” followed by “Confirm equal base/head, captured Git tree identity and clean source status” and “Missing identity records must be completed before CLEAN.”

The two failures explicitly withhold acceptance. The other three neither establish the missing records nor explicitly declare V9 pending, so conditional language is preserved as ambiguity, not credited as a pass or silently relabeled a failure. Thus 5/5 unconditional acceptance is not demonstrated. Under a binary rule counting every unproven acceptance as failure, the same raw answers would be 0 pass / 5 not-demonstrated; the recorded three-way scoring retains the distinction.

This is a **protocol/case evidence-compatibility limitation** and a lingering failure of the frozen unconditional-completion criterion. It is not evidence that the new identity checks are unnecessary, that their helper implementation fails, or that evidence-bound verification-only completion is broken in a live run. The observed failure mechanism differs from installed 6.0’s missing-path problem. These samples cannot establish an improvement or regression effect size for end-to-end completion. No follow-up assessment or source correction was performed in this round.

